Cervical Dysplasia (CIN)
Obstetrics Gynaecology & Womens health
Asymptomatic presentation, Acetowhite changes on colposcopy, High-risk HPV positive

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-010
Reason for Visit: Acute, heavy secondary hemorrhage 5 days following a colposcopy and cervical punch biopsy, causing orthostatic dizziness and significant distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute onset of heavy, bright red vaginal bleeding passing significant clots five days post-cervical biopsy, accompanied by presyncope. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to visualize the cervical vault, clear obstructive clots, and apply robust local hemostatic measures to prevent progressive hypovolemia before the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I had a biopsy five days ago and was fine, but tonight I suddenly started gushing bright red blood and passing massive clots.
History of Present Illness: The patient was overwhelmingly asymptomatic during the pre-malignant latent phases until a positive high-risk Human Papillomavirus (HPV) screen (strain 16) prompted investigation. Five days ago, she underwent colposcopy, which revealed distinctive acetowhite epithelial changes and punctation upon application of 5% acetic acid, resulting in a cervical punch biopsy. Tonight, she developed sudden, heavy vaginal bleeding, soaking through three thick pads in two hours, passing large clots. She reports irregular intermenstrual spotting prior to the biopsy, and abnormal, persistent watery vaginal discharge. Currently complains of dizziness upon standing.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to dizziness; afraid to move to the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; risk of uncontrolled secondary hemorrhage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg | HR: 100 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient appears pale, anxious, and is lying flat in bed.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is soft, non-tender. Speculum examination reveals approximately 50mL of clotted blood in the vaginal vault. Upon clearing the clots, active, brisk oozing is visualized originating from the sharply demarcated biopsy site on the anterior cervical lip. The remainder of the cervix, aside from the procedural trauma, shows no gross exophytic lesions. No purulent discharge noted.
Environmental Observations: Well-lit room, towels prepared by the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No current medications. Not taking aspirin or NSAIDs.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Secondary post-procedural cervical hemorrhage (Status post-colposcopic punch biopsy for CIN) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Pelvic Infection - Ruled out due to absence of fever, lack of purulent cervical discharge, and no severe pelvic peritonitis/tenderness.

Spontaneous Miscarriage - Ruled out by definitive history of recent cervical biopsy as the visual source of active bleeding, and negative urine hCG.

Coagulopathy - Unlikely given the lack of other bleeding diatheses (no petechiae, normal bruising) and a clear local mechanical cause (sloughing of biopsy eschar).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute secondary hemorrhage five days post-cervical biopsy, resulting in mild tachycardia, borderline hypotension, and symptomatic orthostatic dizziness. Direct visualization confirmed active, brisk bleeding from the known procedural site. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely critical to physically clear the obstructing clots and apply direct topical hemostatic agents (Monsel's solution/silver nitrate) to the bleeding vessel. Failing to intervene before the next in-hours period would have resulted in continuous, unchecked blood loss, inevitable hemodynamic decompensation, and required a high-acuity ambulance transfer for hypovolemic shock.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Cleared vaginal clots using ring forceps. Applied silver nitrate sticks directly to the bleeding biopsy site via speculum, followed by firm pressure with a swab for 5 minutes, achieving excellent local hemostasis. Prescribed oral tranexamic acid 1g three times daily for 3 days to stabilize the clot.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent dislodging the newly formed clot, strictly avoid heavy lifting (over 5kg) or strenuous exercise for the next 72 hours; recognizing that physical rest correlates with zero further bleeding will enforce compliance with activity restrictions. To ensure the chemical cautery heals, maintain strict pelvic rest; experiencing an uncomplicated recovery will validate adherence to avoiding tampons and intercourse.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not applicable.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review by phone in 24 hours to ensure bleeding has not recommenced, and formal follow-up to discuss the biopsy histology results next week.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Attend the Emergency Department immediately if brisk, bright red bleeding restarts and soaks through a maxi pad in less than 30 minutes, or if you feel faint or lose consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The bleeding has been arrested locally; however, the scab remains fragile and requires strict rest to heal.
Clinical Course: A dark, coffee-ground-like discharge is expected over the next 48 hours as the silver nitrate clears the system. Active bright red bleeding should not return.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency care if heavy, bright red bleeding resumes or if you experience severe dizziness upon standing.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Around day five to seven after a biopsy, the initial scab can slough off, exposing a raw blood vessel. We have chemically cauterized that specific vessel to stop the bleeding and provided medication to help your body form a stronger, permanent clot.
Actionable Steps: Rest completely in bed for the remainder of the evening. Take the tranexamic acid exactly as prescribed to strengthen the clot, and expect some dark, gritty discharge from the treatment.

